# Ontology Function Workbench

Use this skill when generating or editing a function in the VSCode Function Workbench. The output is a TypeScript default async function that returns an `OntologyTransaction`; it does not commit the transaction itself.

## Runtime contract

- Import `client` and `clientReady` only from `./client`; do not edit, replace, or generate `client.ts`. Its connection configuration comes from the current page location.
- Call `await clientReady`, then `const tx = client.beginTransaction()`, add every changed instance with `tx.add(...)`, and `return tx`.
- Use the selected Capability's generated API code and types exactly as supplied by `ontology.generated.d.ts`.
- Do not edit `ontology.generated.d.ts` or `__generated_user__.d.ts`.

## Imported resources

The resource-import panel generates one read-only definition per imported ontology under the `types` directory; `ontology.generated.d.ts` is the read-only index. AIP receives a machine-readable resource manifest in `codeEditor.ontologyResources` and the merged declarations in `codeEditor.ontologyTypes`.

- Use `ontologyResources` first for exact object API codes, fields, generated data/`LoadedInstance` types, relation owners and targets. Use `externalResources` to see the imported data-source keys, table keys, MetaHub collection keys and tool keys. Use `ontologyTypes` for the complete TypeScript signatures for all three resource kinds.
- Treat these contexts as the source of truth. Do not invent API codes from display labels or create any import statement for these global declarations. This also applies to `client.dataSource` and `client.metahub`; never import a generated declaration from `./types`.
- Do not edit generated definitions. When the user changes imported resources, use the newly generated definitions and adjust only business function files.

### Loaded instance properties

An ontology `LoadedInstance` is an instance wrapper, not a flattened backend row. Business properties are **only** under `instance.properties`; its direct members are runtime metadata such as `$primaryKey`, `$apiName`, `$revision` and `links`.

```ts
// Correct: use the exact generated property API code.
const documentName = document.properties.name;
const sourceDocumentUrl = document.properties.url;

// Wrong: never generate document.name, document.url or document.data.name.
document.set("preview_url", pdfUrl);
```

Use bracket notation when the generated property API code is not a valid TypeScript identifier. Never guess a property name: read it from `ontologyResources` / `ontologyTypes`.

### Data sources and MetaHub

External resources are global generated client APIs. Their collection, table and tool names must be copied exactly from `ontologyTypes` (and can be cross-checked in `externalResources`); they are not IP addresses, resource IDs, or display-name guesses.

```ts
// Data-source rows are ordinary typed rows, not ontology instances.
const customers = await client.dataSource.销售数据库.customers.query({
  page: 1,
  pageSize: 20, // Keep pageSize at or below 200.
  where: { status: "active" },
  orderBy: { createdAt: "desc" },
});
for (const customer of customers.data) {
  console.log(customer.id, customer.name); // No `.properties` on data-source rows.
}

// MetaHub input and output are generated from the selected tool schema.
const pdfResult = await client.metahub.Office工具.office转pdf格式({
  document_name: document.properties.name,
  source_document_url: document.properties.url,
});
document.set("preview_url", pdfResult.pdf_document_url);
tx.add(document);
```

Do not call Fabric endpoints directly, do not use legacy generated keys such as `_192168...`, and do not hide an unknown MetaHub response behind `as { pdf_url?: string; url?: string }`. Use the exact generated input field and output field names. A MetaHub call does not itself produce an ontology transaction; add the changed ontology instance to `tx` after applying `set()`.

## Target-selection rule

Creation is the only write operation that may omit an instance argument. For update, upsert, deletion, and every Links change, require the caller to pass the selected loaded instances. Never find a target inside the function by display name, an arbitrary property, or a hard-coded primary key.

Every transaction function returns `Promise<OntologyTransaction>` and ends with `return tx`; never use `Promise<void>`. For creation, expand every generated object property into an explicit parameter with its generated type and requiredness, then assemble the object passed to `create`. Do not accept `data: Documents` or any other generated object as a black-box data parameter. Do not use `Partial`, assign a second `rid`, or make up empty strings, counters, statuses, or other default values.

Use `DocumentsLoadedInstance[]` below as a placeholder for the generated loaded-instance type of the actual object.

```ts
// Create: each generated property is a caller-supplied parameter.
export default async function executeAction(
  primaryKey: string,
  name?: string,
  category?: string
): Promise<OntologyTransaction> {
  await clientReady;
  const tx = client.beginTransaction();
  const doc = client.objects.Documents.create(primaryKey, { name, category });
  tx.add(doc);
  return tx;
}

// Update: the caller passes the selected instances.
documents.forEach((document: DocumentsLoadedInstance) => {
  document.set("name", name);
  document.set("code", code);
  tx.add(document);
});

// Upsert: use the caller-provided instance key; do not infer a target by name.
documents.forEach((document: DocumentsLoadedInstance) => {
  const doc = client.objects.Documents.upsert(document.$primaryKey, { name, code });
  tx.add(doc);
});

// Delete: delete the caller-provided instance.
documents.forEach((document: DocumentsLoadedInstance) => {
  document.delete();
  tx.add(document);
});
```

## Links

For a relationship mutation, require both the selected source and target instances. Function Workbench has no `changeRelation` API: use only the generated typed relation accessor.

Relation parameters are always required generated loaded instances. Never accept identifiers such as `chunkIds`, never call `get()` or `fetch()` to turn IDs into relation targets, and never make a relation parameter optional. Read the generated definitions to determine which instance owns the relation accessor.

```ts
documents.forEach((document: DocumentsLoadedInstance) => {
  tags.forEach((tag: TagsLoadedInstance) => document.links.relatedTags.add(tag));
  tx.add(document);
});

// Removing a link has the same parameter rule.
documents.forEach((document: DocumentsLoadedInstance) => {
  tags.forEach((tag: TagsLoadedInstance) => document.links.relatedTags.remove(tag));
  tx.add(document);
});
```

When the target is an array, call the accessor once for each selected instance. Do not spread the array into `add` or `remove`.

```ts
for (const chunk of chunks) {
  document.links.分片_文档.add(chunk);
}
```

If the generated declaration exposes a relation as only `{ fetch(): Promise<unknown> }`, it is not an editable relation accessor. Do not use a type assertion or another API to bypass that error; correct the imported resource's relation target definition, regenerate the declarations, then generate the function.

`add` and `remove` also accept a primary-key string at runtime, but generated Function Workbench code must use the caller-provided loaded instance. Never pass a plain property object as a link target. For create-and-link requests, create the new object, apply the accessor on whichever side owns it according to `ontologyResources`, confirm the signature in `ontologyTypes`, and add every changed source instance to the transaction. Do not guess or hard-code a relation direction.

## Delete documents

Deletion receives the selected loaded instances. Do not query IDs, cast a reference instance, or synthesize an object before deleting it.

```ts
export default async function executeAction(
  documents: DocumentsLoadedInstance[]
): Promise<OntologyTransaction> {
  await clientReady;
  const tx = client.beginTransaction();

  documents.forEach((document) => {
    document.delete();
    tx.add(document);
  });

  return tx;
}
```

## Lifecycle functions and argument mapping

Instance lifecycle registration uses `instance.on(trigger, { functionApiCode, args })`.
`args` keys must exactly match the published target function's parameter names. Do not remove `args` or assume a target function always names its parameter `document` or accepts positional automatic injection.

```ts
document.on("UPDATED", {
  functionApiCode: "onDocumentUpdated",
  args: {
    document: { $source: "instance" },
    event: { $source: "event" },
    // Alternatively map individual generated properties to target parameters:
    // oldName: { $source: "before", field: "name" },
    // newName: { $source: "after", field: "name" },
  },
});
```

Bindings are resolved by the lifecycle dispatcher from the committed event. `instance` is restored as the selected generated LoadedInstance; `event` contains `before`, `after`, and per-field `changes` with `oldValue` and `newValue`. Plain JSON values are fixed literals captured at registration. Do not pass an SDK instance or `instance.properties` as a literal and assume it will refresh when the event fires. Do not implement a JavaScript expression evaluator or interpolate strings in mappings.

The SDK and Function Runtime serialize registrations; this repository does not contain the Fabric lifecycle dispatcher. Do not claim a successful build or registration has executed the target function. Check the automation reference for implementation limits. Repeating the same trigger/function binding within a pending instance replaces its argument mapping; durable deduplication, task retries, loop protection and function-deletion dependencies belong to the backend.

When editing through MCP, read `vscode_get_context` and its bundled `guidance` first; use `vscode_get_guidance` for the `function-sdk` and `automation` references. MCP applies exact-text edits through `vscode_apply_changes`; the AIP payload below applies only when replying through AIP.

## Code-edit response

When replying through AIP, emit the required `ontology-code-actions` payload before prose for every code modification. Keep the edit minimal, preserve unrelated code, and never replace an existing file wholesale. Put the complete operation in the existing default `executeAction`; do not leave it empty and add a second exported function or duplicate generated ontology types with local interfaces.

The UI treats model output as untrusted. It first simulates all exact-text edits against the current workspace, then validates the default entry point, transaction lifecycle, generated object and relation names, relation ownership, required `LoadedInstance` parameters, and protected files. Only a valid payload is applied once. The applied edit is reversible through Undo; an invalid payload is shown as not applied with the validation reason.

Read the developer-facing reference at `docs/fabric-docs/docs/function-sdk.md` when detailed query, transaction, or relation behavior is needed.
